Page 387 biti: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|sl|sla-pro|*byti}}, from {{der|sl|ine-pro|*bʰuH-}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{sl-IPA|bíti}}
* {{hyphenation|sl|bí|ti}}

====Verb====
{{sl-verb|bíti|impf}}

# {{lb|sl|intransitive}} to [[be]], to [[exist]]
#: {{uxi|sl|Trgovina '''je''' za kavarno.|The store is behind the cafe}}
# {{lb|sl|transitive|copulative}} {{non-gloss|Used to connect a noun to an adjective, noun or passive [[past participle]] that describes it (equivalent to all English senses and functions as a [[copula]]; subject having the state, quality, identity, nature, role, etc., specified.)}}
#: {{uxi|sl|On '''je''' moj prijatelj.|He '''is''' my friend.}}
# {{lb|sl|intransitive}} [[there be]]
#: {{synonyms|sl|obstājati}}
#: {{ux|sl|'''Bil''' je kralj, ki je vladal devetim deželam.|'''There was''' a king which ruled over nine lands.}}
#: {{ux|sl|Na sredi parcele '''je''' hiša.|'''There is''' a house in the centre of the lot.}}
# {{lb|sl|intransitive|impersonal}} to [[be]] ''(see usage notes)''
#: {{ux|sl|Očitno je '''bilo''', da ni imel pojma o tem, kar govori.|It '''was''' clear that he had no idea about what he was telling.}}
# {{lb|sl|elliptically}} ''Taking the form of another part of a sentence:''
## ''a binding participle''
##* {{uxi|sl|Saj '''boš''' en kozarček, ne?|You '''will drink''' a glass [of a drink], right?}}
##: {{uxi|sl|Kaj '''bomo''' pa zdaj?|What '''are we going to do''' now?}}
## ''a part of a subject''
##: {{uxi|sl|Ta stol '''je''' iz lesa.|This chair '''is made''' out of wood.}}
## {{lb|sl|modal|impersonal|with infinitive}} ''a [[modal verb]] or [[predicative]] denoting possibility or obligation; often translated as passive''
##: {{uxi|sl|Žive duše ni '''bilo''' videti|There was not a single person '''to be''' seen.|lit=(One) '''could''' not see a single soul}}
## {{lb|sl|in conditional mood|colloquial}} ''verb {{l|sl|imẹ́ti}}''
##: {{uxi|sl|Jaz '''bi''' plišastega medvedka.|I '''would like to have''' a teddy bear.}}
# {{lb|sl|intransitive}} to [[cost]]
#: {{uxi|sl|Torbica je '''bila''' sto evrov.|The purse '''cost''' a hundered euros.}}
# {{lb|sl|intransitive|expressive}} to [[feel]], to [[want]]{{sl-ref|SSKJ}}
#: {{uxi|sl|'''Bilo''' mu je, da bi zavriskal.|He '''wanted''' to shout out with joy.}}
# {{lb|sl|intransitive|expressive|followed by {{l|sl|da bi}}}} to be [[worth]]
#* {{quote-web|sl|url=https://www.dt-rs.si/158/govor-generalnega-drzavnega-tozilca-na-posvetu-otrok-in-pasti-sodobnega-casa|title=Govor generalnega državnega tožilca na posvetu "Otrok in pasti sodobnega časa"|date=2015-4-2|accessdate=2023-02-07|work=Državno tožilstvo republike Slovenije|passage=O aktualnosti teme pa '''ni''' da bi govoril, saj je pravzaprav večna: ni bilo časa, ki s sabo ne bi prinašal, ob vseh drugih, tudi novih pasti, problemov in vprašanj.|translation=It '''is not worth''' talking about the topicality of this topic because it is actually timeless: there was not a time that would not bring, in addition to all other, also new traps, problems and questions.}}
# to [[hold]], to be [[true]], [[apply]]{{sl-ref|SSKJ}}
#: {{uxi|sl|Zakon '''je''' za vse.|Laws '''apply''' to everyone.}}
# {{lb|sl|auxiliary|present forms}} ''Used to form [[preterite]] tense.''
# {{lb|sl|auxiliary|present forms followed by l-participle}} ''Used to form [[pluperfect]] tense.''
# {{lb|sl|auxiliary|future forms}} ''Used to form [[future tense]].''
# {{lb|sl|auxiliary|conditional form}} ''Used to form present [[conditional mood]].''
# {{lb|sl|auxiliary|conditional form followed by l-participle}} ''Used to form [[preterite]] [[conditional mood]].''
# {{lb|sl|auxiliary}} ''Used to form passive voice.''

=====Usage notes=====
This verb is very hard to translate in English as it can form many specific formations that are not present in English and often require complete restructuring of the sentence. Additionally, it also has many irregularities and additional forms regarding conjugation. Both of these are detailed here.

======Impersonal be======
Impersonal be can form many specific forms that often have to be translated together with the surrounding words, often changing the whole structure of a sentence:
* {{uxi|sl|Očitno je '''bilo''', da ni imel pojma o tem, kar govori.|It '''was''' clear that he had no idea about what he was telling.}}
* {{uxi|sl|Veliko kruha '''je''' še.|'''There is''' a lot of bread (left)}}
* {{uxi|sl|Strah me '''je'''.|I '''am''' scared.}}
* {{uxi|sl|Žal mi je '''bilo'''.|I '''regretted''' it.}}

In the first example, also given in the definitions, there is no problem translating the verb as the impersonality can be represented by impersonal {{l|en|it}}. In the second example, the form {{l|en|there be}} can be used to convey the meaning. However, in the last two examples, translation is not that simple.

In the third example, word ''{{lang|sl|strah}}'' means "{{l|en|fear}}" {{q|noun}}, ''{{lang|sl|me}}'' means "{{l|en|I}}" {{q|accusative case}} and ''{{lang|sl|je}}'' is the impersonal form of ''biti''. Therefore, if one would literally translate this sentence, it would sound "Fear me is", which would not be understandable. In this case, the correct translation would be to convert "I" into the subject of the sentence, correctly conjugate the verb "{{l|en|be}}" and find a suitable adjective that would convey the same meaning, in this case "{{l|en|scared}}".

In the fourth example, ''{{lang|sl|žal}}'' means "{{l|en|unfortunately}}", ''{{lang|sl|mi}}'' means "{{l|en|I}}" {{q|dative case}}, and ''{{lang|sl|je bilo}}'' is the [[preterite]] impersonal form ''biti''. In this case, the verb has to be changed to the one that conveys the same meaning, and I has to be again converted into the subject.

These forms are not separate verbs as these forms are quite common and most of them are easily translatable ({{uxi|sl|'''Bilo''' je mrzlo.|It '''was''' cold.}}). This kind of untranslatable formations only appears in cases where dative or accusative are present and can in some cases also extend to the personal be, e.g. {{uxi|sl|Same kosti '''so''' ga.|He '''is''' skinny.|lit=The bones are him.}}, which is easier to understand, but still weird to say.

This problem is similar to that in German, e.g. with the word {{l|de|kalt}}.

======Additional usage notes regarding conjugation======
The verb has three additional forms that are not common for other verbs: future forms, negative present indicative forms and conditional forms.

The future forms bear no stress when the verb is used as an auxiliary verb and stressed when it is a full-lexical form of the verb. In common speech, however, it is common to also form the future tense as with any other verb, i.e. unstressed forms followed by l-participle ({{lang|sl|bom bil}}). This form is not considered to be correct by SP{{sl-ref|SP}}. In some dialects, future forms also have its special negative form, {{l|sl|naum}}/{{l|sl|noum}}/{{l|sl|nbom}}.

The conditional form is used only as an auxiliary verb, except in cases with [[ellipsis]] (sense 5). Even when one wants to form conditional of ''biti'', l-participle has to be included (''bi bil''). The verb also has no distinction between present and past conditional; both use only one l-participle. It usually bears no stress, but in negative form, the stress can be transferred from ''ne'' (ne bȉ), however the more common form is as usual (nȅ bi).

Present forms are stressed as a full-lexical verb and unstressed as auxiliary verb.

All unstressed forms can also bear stress when they have the stress in the sentence.

